WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.170
Operations teams do not distrust automation because it is inaccurate. They distrust it because they cannot see what it did.

2
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:18.440
What it cannot do is establish that a person agreed, that they were who they claimed to be, and that the agreement can be shown to someone else weeks later.

3
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:26.550
Telemetry in, a proposal out, and a boundary in the middle that the system cannot cross on its own.

4
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:35.140
The guardrails tab states what is enforced and what is not, rather than implying everything is.

5
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:45.050
A policy evaluator decides whether an action is permissible at all, and anything that would write is held in a queue until a person acts.

6
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:52.960
A system whose central claim is that its choices are provable after the fact has written its choices down.

7
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:59.710
The domain model, with every entity named against the requirement that asked for it.

8
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.500
Every requirement traced to the thing that satisfies it, in the system's own command centre.

9
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.530
Nine operator roles, each taken from the story that names them rather than invented for a persona deck.

10
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:28.250
One decision record commits to multi-factor authentication on the approving account; another binds approval to a verified identity rather than to a session.

11
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:37.620
Both are published on the record rather than left out. The system proves who agreed to a change, not that the change was the right one.

12
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.840
Every claim on the page is pinned to a commit you can open.
